      "id": "s2"
    },
    {
      "narration": "How you split your assets is called asset allocation.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-allocation",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · asset allocation"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s3"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The choice matters enormously. Over thirty years, a dollar in stocks beat a dollar in cash eight to one.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "bank",
          "big": "$8 vs $1",
          "label": "stocks vs cash, 30 years",
          "props": [
            "chartUp",
            "coin"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s4"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Stocks are financial assets that tend to grow with the economy.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-finasset",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · financial asset"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s5"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Cash feels safe, but after inflation it quietly shrinks for decades.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "ink",
          "line": "Safe cash shrinks",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "chartDown"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s6"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Real wealth is built by what you own, not what you hold in cash.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-wealth",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · wealth"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s7"
    },
    {
      "narration": "What you own, far more than what you earn, quietly decides whether your wealth grows.",
